WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.800
Can a dry summer change the carbon dioxide in the air?

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.780
To answer that, we have to follow water, not carbon.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.700
The first film showed the air as a tub of carbon.

4
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.340
The oak's green arrow drained it every summer.

5
00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.320
Now follow the oak's water instead.

6
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:23.903
Rain falls on the yard.

7
00:00:25.123 --> 00:00:26.143
That is precipitation.

8
00:00:27.623 --> 00:00:30.423
Some of it soaks into the ground, which is infiltration.

9
00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:33.703
Some flows downhill to a stream.

10
00:00:34.943 --> 00:00:35.943
That is runoff.

11
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:39.147
The oak's roots take water from the soil.

12
00:00:40.387 --> 00:00:42.027
It moves up the stem to the leaves.

13
00:00:43.247 --> 00:00:47.147
Then it leaves through tiny openings in the leaves, as water vapor.

14
00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:49.567
That is transpiration.

15
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:55.050
Water also evaporates from the pond, and most of all from the ocean.

16
00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.890
High up, the vapor cools into tiny droplets that form clouds.

17
00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.350
That is condensation.

18
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.030
Then rain falls again.

19
00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:07.933
Plants move a lot of this water.

20
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:13.773
One study estimated that nearly two thirds of the water moving from land into the

21
00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.413
air goes through plants.

22
00:01:16.653 --> 00:01:18.453
The estimate has a wide range.

23
00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:21.177
Now zoom into one leaf.

24
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:27.917
Carbon dioxide comes in through the same tiny openings that water vapor goes out of.

25
00:01:28.997 --> 00:01:30.597
One door serves both cycles.

26
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:37.360
Inside the leaf, photosynthesis uses water and carbon dioxide together to make

27
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.440
sugar. The water came up from the roots.

28
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.840
The carbon dioxide came in through the door.

29
00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:46.483
Now a hot, dry summer.

30
00:01:47.723 --> 00:01:52.123
The soil dries out, and the oak closes its tiny openings to save water.

31
00:01:53.443 --> 00:01:55.643
What happens to the green arrow on the air tub?

32
00:01:57.023 --> 00:01:58.023
Pause and predict.

33
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:10.177
It gets thinner. With the openings nearly closed, less carbon dioxide gets in.

34
00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.417
So the oak takes less carbon out of the air.

35
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.740
This happens in real forests.

36
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.300
In 2003, Europe had a very hot, dry summer.

37
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:27.160
One study estimated that plants there took in about 30 percent less carbon than

38
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.160
usual.

39
00:02:28.583 --> 00:02:31.023
One thing in this picture does not cycle.

40
00:02:32.543 --> 00:02:36.003
Carbon and water go around and around, used again and again.

41
00:02:37.343 --> 00:02:43.283
Energy does not. It comes in as sunlight and leaves as heat, so plants need new

42
00:02:43.283 --> 00:02:44.363
sunlight every day.

43
00:02:45.417 --> 00:02:47.617
The two cycles are linked at the leaf.

44
00:02:48.857 --> 00:02:53.817
Water going out and carbon dioxide coming in pass through the same tiny openings.

45
00:02:55.097 --> 00:02:57.697
So a change in one cycle can change the other.
